            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Portuguese for later, then the Portuguese for finally."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: entretanto",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "entretanto — \"meanwhile, in the meantime; however\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eu fiz o jantar; entretanto, o meu irmão pôs a mesa. I made dinner; meanwhile my brother set out the plates and glasses."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In formal writing it can also mean ‘however’; in Brazil that is the main sense."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "entretanto joins entre \"between\" and tanto \"so much\", from Latin inter \"between\" and tantum \"so much\" (Lewis & Short): the time in between."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
